+## 6i. Task 14: larger input waists, intracavity (thermo-optic) modulation, 100 ms limits
+
+`scripts/t14_waist_mod.js`, `scripts/t14_analysis.py`, `scripts/t14_compact.py`, `scripts/t14_outlook.py` → `results/t14/`
+(`table.md`, `summary.json`, `compact.json`, `outlook.json`). Task 11 cells (5 / 10 / 15 / 20 mm, 8 inclusions r 25 µm, facet 11),
+set B, 0.3 dB/cm, 30 layouts, 3.3 µm (±81 pm) and 1.53 µm (±35 pm). Launch positions now span 0.45 × the input facet of
+each cell (Task 11: 250 µm for all sizes), so the 75 µm design differs slightly from Task 11/12 (10 mm, R 99.5 %, 100 ms:
+CH₄ 1.50 against 1.35 ppb).
+
+Modulation model (first order, wave-consistent): each beamlet keeps its traced route; a modulation of the slab index by δ
+in a region adds the phase k₀ δ ℓ, ℓ the route's length inside the region, and the time average of a sine modulation is
+exactly J₀(k₀ δ ℓ) per route (product over independently modulated regions). Regions: the inclusions (r 25 µm) or 75 µm
+heated spots at their centres, independent or common; a central zone of half the cell area; the whole membrane. Route
+geometry changes are sub-diffraction and left out. dn_eff/dT ≈ (1 − Γ) 2.5e-5 /K = 1.1e-5 /K at 3.3 µm.
+
+Findings (3.3 µm; 100 ms, referenced every 3 s, drift 0.1 rad/s, NEP 14 pW/√Hz, two couplers 8 dB):
+1. Larger waist: N 86 → 235 / 384 / 736 (75 → 150 / 250 / 400 µm), static fringe down 2–3× (10 mm: 1.6e-6 → 7.9e-7;
+   15 mm: 1.2e-6 → 4.5e-7), but the transmission falls (10 mm: 4.0 → 2.6 %; 15 mm 2.0 → 1.0 %), so the 100 ms limit improves
+   only where the fringe dominates (10 mm, R 99.5 %: 1.50 → 1.21 ppb CH₄) and is worse in the 15–20 mm cells.
+2. Modulating the inclusions does almost nothing: a route spends 0–100 µm inside the eight 25 µm disks, so even δ = 3e-3
+   (≈ 270 K) gives k₀δℓ < 1; 75 µm spots help ≤ 2× at δ = 3e-3. What matters is the route length inside the modulated region.
+3. Modulating the whole membrane (or half of it) works like a deep wavelength dither on the fringe phases while the laser
+   line stays fixed (no line smearing): δ = 1e-4 (±9 K) lowers the static fringe 10× (10 mm: 1.6e-6 → 1.5e-7), δ = 1e-3 30×.
+   Without any input dither, wavelength + membrane δ 1e-4 gives 1.1e-6 (10 mm), close to the full input dither alone.
+4. At 100 ms the limit is then set by (a) the boxcar remainder of the 1 kHz dithers (Task 12 model; ≈ 2e-3 of the undithered
+   fringe) and (b) the detector. Synchronous averaging over whole dither periods removes (a); the power on the detector
+   (10 mW × T × 8 dB loss ≈ 30–60 µW) then sets the floor.
+5. Best 100 ms limits (CH₄ / C₂H₆, ppb, median; membrane δ 1e-4, synchronous averaging): 10 mm, R 99.5 %: 7.0 / 8.9 at 1 mW,
+   0.74 / 0.94 at 10 mW, 0.19 / 0.24 at 50 mW; R 97 %: 13 / 17, 1.34 / 1.69, 0.31 / 0.39. Without the membrane modulation
+   (input + wavelength dither only, synchronous): 1.38 / 1.75 (10 mW) and 1.11 / 1.40 (50 mW) at R 99.5 %.
+6. Practicality of δ 1e-4 over a 10 mm membrane: ±9 K at 20–50 Hz; the 0.3 mm gas gaps give h ≈ 170 W/m²K, τ ≈ 6–7 ms, and
+   ≈ 13 mW/K for the whole membrane (≈ 0.1–0.25 W absorbed: CW pump or heaters outside the beam paths; half-area zone halves
+   it). Side effects: gas-density and line-strength modulation (±3 % at ±9 K) at f_mod, averaged and calibrated by the
+   referencing; thermal stress of the suspended film. Not yet modelled: route deflection by thermal gradients.
+
 ## 6g. Task 12: 100 ms breath measurements (CH₄, C₂H₆, NH₃)
 
